three different ideas of what we should call something in the software.

200
00:11:09,560 --> 00:11:10,560
Jason A. Cullinan.

201
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:11,360
How is it different?

202
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:11,440
Jason A. Cullinan, That's good.

203
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:14,420
How are we going to represent these ideas in our software?

204
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:18,600
Well, it was about this time that my boss actually recommended

205
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:21,220
I read the Domain Driven Design book.

206
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:28,480
I started my programming career actually doing software modeling

207
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:31,080
for geology and geotechnical engineering.

208
00:11:31,220 --> 00:11:33,340
My very first experience of programming

209
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:36,140
was getting in a small 10-seater aircraft,

210
00:11:36,420 --> 00:11:38,860
flying out to a mine site like this,

211
00:11:38,860 --> 00:11:43,160
and spending a week, maybe two weeks,

212
00:11:43,780 --> 00:11:47,060
working with the geologist or the geotechnical engineer,

213
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:50,700
trying to understand how they did what they did.

214
00:11:51,160 --> 00:11:55,720
How did they use the software to model this mine site?

215
00:11:55,840 --> 00:12:00,880
How did they use it to figure out how to cut the berms that you can see here?

216
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:03,540
And what angle to make them so they would be safe?

217
00:12:06,300 --> 00:12:08,680
I read books on geotechnical modeling,

218
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:12,540
so the idea was to take our software

219
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:18,440
and actually implement some of these geotechnical techniques in our software.

220
00:12:19,440 --> 00:12:21,860
We'd code up some experimental ideas,

221
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:25,460
send him an updated release, get his feedback, make improvements,

222
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:28,420
and we'd repeat this process until we had something

223
00:12:28,420 --> 00:12:30,680
that he really felt he could use on a daily basis.

224
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:34,540
And what I really loved about that collaborative kind of process

225
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:38,320
was his mounting enthusiasm as we converged on something

226
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:38,680
that was really important.

227
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:39,720
Something that was really useful for him.

228
00:12:42,820 --> 00:12:47,260
So these were some of the artifacts that we actually brought into the software

229
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:49,600
that are used in geotechnical analysis.

230
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:53,980
And what's fascinating to me is I wrote the original version of this software

231
00:12:53,980 --> 00:12:58,560
back in 1994, and I took this off the web yesterday

232
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:01,360
as something that's still used in the same product today.

233
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:06,320
Software can be long-lived, sometimes more than we would think.

234
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:12,520
The thing that attracted me with domain-driven design

235
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:15,860
was that it seemed to work the same way.

236
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:19,020
It was a simple idea.

237
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:22,320
Collaborate closely with the experts in your business.

238
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:24,240
Come up with a shared language.

239
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,880
Understand how they do what they do

240
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:29,520
and work to represent that in the software.

241
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:37,520
And then as you learn, refactor and refine the...

242
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:43,680
the code to more closely match what the business is doing.

243
00:13:44,860 --> 00:13:46,480
This is a simple idea in theory,

244
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:49,440
but I found it really tricky to pull off on a real project,

245
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:55,480
especially one with tight deadlines and nervous stakeholders.

246
00:13:57,020 --> 00:14:01,720
The book provided a set of coding patterns and techniques for doing this,

247
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:03,540
and while I didn't understand them all,

248
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:05,580
if you've seen this book, it's a big book.

249
00:14:06,480 --> 00:14:07,780
While I didn't understand them all,

250
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:08,660
what I did understand,

251
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:11,400
we started putting into practice on our projects.

252
00:14:12,180 --> 00:14:14,480
Now, you can imagine, based on that email exchange,

253
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:18,800
this caused quite a bit of friction and confusion with our business experts.

254
00:14:18,980 --> 00:14:20,980
They weren't used to this level of collaboration.

255
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:26,520
It went from passive-aggressive email exchanges, like you saw,

256
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:29,280
to actually wanting to talk with them

257
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:31,900
to understand how these things in the business worked.

258
00:14:35,320 --> 00:14:38,440
Not only were they not used to close interaction,

259
00:14:38,440 --> 00:14:39,000
as I mentioned,

260
00:14:39,140 --> 00:14:42,480
but they were also quite disillusioned about this project, as I said.

261
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:45,640
So, this had to change if we were going to succeed, but how?

262
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:49,080
Well, we started small.

263
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:50,700
We started small.

264
00:14:50,820 --> 00:14:54,940
We asked for help on some of the key specific concepts

265
00:14:54,940 --> 00:14:56,100
that we were working on.

266
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:58,760
And one of the key ideas here

267
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:03,720
is to approach from a position of humble curiosity.

268
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:06,720
Technical people often...

269
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:09,640
find that difficult.

270
00:15:09,640 --> 00:15:11,140
I am speaking for myself.

271
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:14,740
And, you know, we think we know everything.

272
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:16,240
Just ask my wife, right?

273
00:15:18,040 --> 00:15:21,040
And so, being able to approach people in the business and say,

274
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:23,540
I don't understand this, but I need to understand this

275
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:25,840
if the software is going to do what you want it to do.

276
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:27,440
Can you explain it to me?

277
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:28,840
Can you give me an example?

278
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:32,540
We asked them to tell stories of how extended warranties

279
00:15:32,540 --> 00:15:35,240
and the claims process work to provide real examples.

280
00:15:35,240 --> 00:15:36,640
And we listened and took notes.

281
00:15:37,220 --> 00:15:38,400
And we asked lots...

282
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:40,200
lots and lots of clarifying questions.

283
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:42,200
So, here's an example of this.

284
00:15:43,300 --> 00:15:47,700
Imagine I went to one of our business people and I said,

285
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:50,600
how does an extended warranty work from a customer perspective?

286
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:52,500
And the answer might go something like this.

287
00:15:53,300 --> 00:15:56,100
David and Claudia have six children living at home.

288
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:58,800
So, there are always a lot of dishes to clean.

289
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:02,100
And on March 10th, after a particularly horrible experience

290
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:07,500
where their current dishwasher just stopped working for good,

291
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:10,700
they purchased a new one at the local appliance store.

292
00:16:10,700 --> 00:16:15,100
They needed a quiet, dependable, high-capacity dishwasher

293
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:18,400
since their last dishwasher had been such a major headache.

294
00:16:20,200 --> 00:16:21,900
So, they chose a KitchenAid dishwasher

295
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:26,600
because it had a good reputation for high quality and few issues.

296
00:16:27,300 --> 00:16:32,400
The dishwasher was on sale for US$1,199

297
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:36,900
and included free installation, saving them US$200 off the regular retail price,

298
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:39,200
and a US$75 installation fee.

299
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:43,900
And it automatically came with a one-year manufacturer warranty from KitchenAid.

300
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:47,000
There's a lot of details in that, right?

301
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,400
So, what we would do is we would frantically take notes

302
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:52,800
and then try to understand what are the key details

303
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:56,100
relative to what we're trying to model here, what we're trying to do.

304
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:02,200
So, I say, well, that sounds good because I understand how they bought this product,

305
00:17:02,200 --> 00:17:04,800
but what about the extended warranty?

306
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:05,700
How does that fit?

307
00:17:06,200 --> 00:17:06,800
And so,

308
00:17:06,900 --> 00:17:11,400
the expert in extended warranties would probably say something like,

309
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:16,800
well, they paid an extra US$82 at the checkout to purchase an extended warranty.

310
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:20,800
And that would cover the KitchenAid dishwasher for another three years

311
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:23,200
after the manufacturer's warranty expires.

312
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
And it would cover that dishwasher for any repairs that needed to happen.

313
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:32,400
And also, they would get a similar replacement dishwasher

314
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:34,100
if their appliance could not be fixed.

315
00:17:35,300 --> 00:17:36,200
That's a wealth of information.

316
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:39,200
That's a wealth of information packed into a very short story.

317
00:17:40,300 --> 00:17:44,800
But what that allowed us to do is to start diagramming this thing out.

318
00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:46,400
So, we would walk over to a whiteboard and say,

319
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:49,100
okay, well, sounds like we have, you call it an extended warranty.

320
00:17:49,600 --> 00:17:51,800
And they say, well, sometimes we call it a contract.

321
00:17:51,800 --> 00:17:54,400
And it's like, okay, well, we have to make a decision.

322
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:56,000
Let's put contract for now,

323
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:59,700
but maybe it's going to need to be called extended warranty in the software.

324
00:17:59,700 --> 00:18:00,800
We're not sure.

325
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,100
Or maybe something else.

326
00:18:02,700 --> 00:18:04,800
Maybe extended warranty is too specific.

327
00:18:04,800 --> 00:18:06,100
We'll have to figure that out.

328
00:18:06,200 --> 00:18:08,500
And then we heard you say some other things.

329
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:14,200
So, we heard you say that there's a, well, you said they pulled it from their appliance store.

330
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:17,900
That sounds like that's a company that we have and a client of ours.

331
00:18:17,900 --> 00:18:21,800
Let's, maybe we'll call that a client company for now, just tentatively.

332
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:24,500
Like, in the U.S., it would be a company like Best Buy.

333
00:18:24,500 --> 00:18:26,200
And they said, yeah, that kind of makes sense.

334
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:27,800
Well, what about the dishwasher?

335
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:28,400
What is that?

336
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:31,000
Well, that's the product that's covered by the warranty.

337
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:31,800
Okay.

338
00:18:32,200 --> 00:18:34,400
Well, how about we call that the covered product?

339
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:36,100
And that's where we put the details about that.

340
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:37,000
Okay.

341
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:37,800
That makes sense.

342
00:18:38,500 --> 00:18:39,100
Well, what else?

343
00:18:39,100 --> 00:18:40,600
Well, we need the customer information.

344
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:43,000
We need their name and address and information like that.

345
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:43,300
Okay.

346
00:18:43,300 --> 00:18:45,300
Well, let's call that customer information.

347
00:18:46,100 --> 00:18:51,200
Well, now we have the beginnings of some kind of thing that we could represent in software.

348
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:53,600
So, we're trying to start to bridge that gap.

349
00:18:54,100 --> 00:19:02,800
And then we would draw a lot of other diagrams and come up with other examples to try and figure out how these business concepts work.

350
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:04,900
And what are the relationships between them?

351
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:11,100
And, like I said, we'd experiment with different ways, different names for things, different ways of expressing that.

352
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:24,500
Now, sometimes our business analysts use sticky notes to map out these stories that the business was telling us.

353
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:35,400
Maybe they would do it in real time as the business people were telling the story or maybe they would do it after the fact to provide a shared visual understanding of how these things work.

354
00:19:35,400 --> 00:19:35,900
So,

355
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:43,200
what we would do is we'd have a lively debate about these business concepts and how they relate to each other.

356
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:48,200
The idea is we're trying to do iterative collaborative design.

357
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:52,700
So, we're trying to come up with something that's an initial idea and then go from there.

358
00:19:52,700 --> 00:20:02,700
So, another example of this is how might we handle the repair process when something does go wrong on an appliance like that dishwasher you were just talking about?

359
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:05,600
And they might say something like, well,

360
00:20:06,200 --> 00:20:11,300
the customer calls our 1-800 number and they speak to a customer service representative.

361
00:20:11,300 --> 00:20:14,100
We call them CSRs at our call center.

362
00:20:14,100 --> 00:20:21,400
The CSR asks the customer to provide either the warranty ID or the brand and serial number of the appliance.

363
00:20:21,400 --> 00:20:26,700
Either one of those will allow us to look up the contract in the system.

364
00:20:26,700 --> 00:20:35,100
And from there, if we find out that the covered product failed during the time that the contract is active based on the effective date and the expiration date,

365
00:20:35,100 --> 00:20:36,100
then we know that it's not going to be a good thing.

366
00:20:36,100 --> 00:20:40,720
that this is a valid claim.

367
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:45,120
So then the CSR would open a new claim and record all the relevant info.

368
00:20:45,120 --> 00:20:50,120
They would create a purchase order, send it to our Acme Appliance Repair Company that

369
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:53,500
does all our appliance repairs.

370
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:57,400
Once the repair is completed, that company would update the purchase order and send it

371
00:20:57,400 --> 00:21:05,660
back to us and we'd use that to get all the financials to send to accounting.

372
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:09,560
So we would do things like map that out on sticky notes.

373
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:14,260
Kenny will be talking about this type of technique later today, have different ways of doing

374
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:20,220
these kinds of collaborations, being able to see how a process works.

375
00:21:20,220 --> 00:21:21,500
And we'd iterate over that.

376
00:21:21,500 --> 00:21:27,020
We'd assume that the first idea that we're coming up with is probably somewhat naive,

377
00:21:27,020 --> 00:21:31,200
somewhat superficial, but we can iterate over that and see if we can improve it over time

378
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:34,700
as we work through it.

379
00:21:34,700 --> 00:21:35,640
Okay.

380
00:21:35,640 --> 00:21:42,860
Another thing that was critical for us, so not only humility in our curiosity and not

381
00:21:42,860 --> 00:21:46,840
only iterative and collaborative design working together, but short feedback loops.

382
00:21:46,840 --> 00:21:52,100
And I think this is true whether you're doing domain-driven design or any kind of process

383
00:21:52,100 --> 00:21:57,320
is working in small batches and having short feedback loops.

384
00:21:57,320 --> 00:21:58,380
This is critical.

385
00:21:58,380 --> 00:21:59,880
So what do I mean by that?

386
00:21:59,880 --> 00:22:04,360
Well, what we did is we usually started with understanding and implementing what you might

387
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:05,440
call the happy path.

388
00:22:05,440 --> 00:22:09,120
The simple option first.

389
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:16,040
And then quickly pivoted to experimenting with the more valuable, higher risk complex cases.

390
00:22:16,040 --> 00:22:20,120
I think of it a little bit like crash testing our ideas, right?

391
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:22,480
Crash testing our model.

392
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:27,280
We'd come up with some initial ideas, developers would code them up fairly quickly.

393
00:22:27,280 --> 00:22:31,160
And then we'd walk through them with our warranty and claim experts to see how well they work.

394
00:22:31,160 --> 00:22:35,320
So at this point in the process, we're not necessarily right.

395
00:22:35,320 --> 00:22:35,440
Okay.

396
00:22:35,440 --> 00:22:36,840
We're writing production code.

397
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:41,100
We're writing experimental code to see if we can understand how the concepts work before

398
00:22:41,100 --> 00:22:44,680
we actually commit to writing production code.

399
00:22:44,680 --> 00:22:50,040
When you don't know yet the right way to implement something, trying a few options is a really

400
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:55,900
smart way to figure out the best option, right, rather than committing to the first idea you

401
00:22:55,900 --> 00:23:00,980
have, which is probably not going to be the best idea.

402
00:23:00,980 --> 00:23:02,940
So short feedback loops.

403
00:23:02,940 --> 00:23:05,320
And I'm talking days here, not weeks.

404
00:23:05,440 --> 00:23:11,800
And showing unfinished work as part of this as well.

405
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:18,880
Now, related to this is the idea of as we were going through this, like I mentioned,

406
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:23,400
as they're telling the story, what we're trying to do is identify key concepts that we would

407
00:23:23,400 --> 00:23:25,160
need to express.

408
00:23:25,160 --> 00:23:29,640
So in that story, I told you, okay, so we have this KitchenAid dishwasher.

409
00:23:29,640 --> 00:23:31,060
That's a covered product.

410
00:23:31,060 --> 00:23:32,900
We have a manufacturer's warranty.

411
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:34,280
That's going to be important.

412
00:23:34,280 --> 00:23:35,320
How are we going to represent that?

413
00:23:35,320 --> 00:23:40,680
How are we going to represent that in the software?

414
00:23:40,680 --> 00:23:42,500
There's this $1,199.

415
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:44,420
That's the cost of the product.

416
00:23:44,420 --> 00:23:46,100
But that's a separate cost.

417
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:47,600
That's a purchase price.

418
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:50,400
But it's the purchase price of the dishwasher.

419
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:54,920
It's not the purchase price for the extended warranty, right?

420
00:23:54,920 --> 00:23:59,460
These were all things that we kind of struggled with because in the code, or if people just

421
00:23:59,460 --> 00:24:02,360
talked about purchase price, we didn't know what they were talking about.

422
00:24:02,360 --> 00:24:04,320
Are you talking about what you paid for the dishwasher?

423
00:24:04,320 --> 00:24:05,320
Are you talking about what you paid for the dishwasher?

424
00:24:05,320 --> 00:24:06,780
Are you talking about what you paid for the actual warranty?

425
00:24:06,780 --> 00:24:09,240
Because those are two separate things.

426
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:13,560
We had to distinguish between those two things, and in the code, we had to make sure that

427
00:24:13,560 --> 00:24:16,440
we called them different things as well.

428
00:24:16,440 --> 00:24:20,440
We had other ideas that are expressed here, like terms and conditions.

429
00:24:20,440 --> 00:24:21,440
What is that?

430
00:24:21,440 --> 00:24:22,440
What does that mean?

431
00:24:22,440 --> 00:24:23,960
We didn't know.

432
00:24:23,960 --> 00:24:28,520
And then how do we express this idea of a period of three years past the manufacturer's

433
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:34,480
warranty so that we could avoid fraud and the kinds of things like that?

434
00:24:34,480 --> 00:24:35,160
And then...

435
00:24:35,160 --> 00:24:40,240
Another question that came up in that story was, well, how do we handle replacements?

436
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:42,780
How do we handle that situation where...

437
00:24:42,780 --> 00:24:51,120
Well, firstly, how do we know how to evaluate if the product couldn't be fixed, right?

438
00:24:51,120 --> 00:24:52,780
Who makes that decision?

439
00:24:52,780 --> 00:24:55,560
And then from there, how do we then pivot to handling a replacement?

440
00:24:55,560 --> 00:24:59,520
We had to figure all that out and express that in the software as well.

441
00:24:59,520 --> 00:25:05,040
So a lot of complexity in terms of what was going on here, and we needed to express that.

442
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:09,600
So an example of this was, expanding on this previous one, was the idea that a contract

443
00:25:09,600 --> 00:25:14,720
had a claim, could have multiple claims associated with it.

444
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:19,600
And then these claims had one or more repair purchase orders, which was what was talked

445
00:25:19,600 --> 00:25:21,020
about in the story.

446
00:25:21,020 --> 00:25:26,600
And then on those repair purchase orders, we had line items to represent costs associated

447
00:25:26,600 --> 00:25:27,640
with that repair.

448
00:25:27,640 --> 00:25:31,920
So maybe the servicer goes and replaces the soap dispenser in the dishwasher, so there

449
00:25:31,920 --> 00:25:34,880
would be a cost for that soap dispenser.

450
00:25:34,880 --> 00:25:41,260
And also for the labor associated with that.

451
00:25:41,260 --> 00:25:43,040
Now why am I mentioning all these details?

452
00:25:43,040 --> 00:25:47,120
This may, you know, 9.30 in the morning, this may seem like a little bit overwhelming.

453
00:25:47,120 --> 00:25:49,260
I apologize for that.

454
00:25:49,260 --> 00:25:54,260
But I want you to get a sense of when you are working in a complex domain, it's like

455
00:25:54,260 --> 00:25:55,260
this.

456
00:25:55,260 --> 00:25:59,060
When you start, you can feel overwhelmed by all these details.

457
00:25:59,060 --> 00:26:02,940
And what I'd encourage you is don't feel overwhelmed, right?

458
00:26:02,940 --> 00:26:04,840
Or if you start to feel that way, just remind yourself.

459
00:26:04,840 --> 00:26:07,560
We just need to get to the next step.

460
00:26:07,560 --> 00:26:10,400
And so it's modeling these things in stages.

461
00:26:10,400 --> 00:26:16,040
And what really helped us was this idea of not only short feedback loops, but tying that

462
00:26:16,040 --> 00:26:17,600
to implementation as well.

463
00:26:17,600 --> 00:26:20,200
So rapid and frequent delivery.

464
00:26:20,200 --> 00:26:24,940
What I mean by that is once we had a good enough understanding of how a particular concept

465
00:26:24,940 --> 00:26:28,360
worked, we'd implement it in code as quickly as we could.

466
00:26:28,360 --> 00:26:29,360
We'd try out options.

467
00:26:29,360 --> 00:26:33,980
And once we felt like we understood how it should work, then we'd deliver it as production

468
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:34,820
quality code.

469
00:26:34,840 --> 00:26:37,560
And that's how we got to where we are today, and that's how we got to where we are today.

470
00:26:37,560 --> 00:26:38,560
And that's how we got to where we are today.

471
00:26:38,560 --> 00:26:42,280
And it was primarily our rapid and frequent delivery of these key capabilities that led

472
00:26:42,280 --> 00:26:44,860
to trust with our stakeholders.

473
00:26:44,860 --> 00:26:50,200
They trusted us because they could see that we were actually delivering things.

474
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:57,400
I think on a lot of projects, the tendency for management or stakeholders to emphasize

475
00:26:57,400 --> 00:27:02,600
estimation and forecasting so much is because they're not seeing delivery.

476
00:27:02,600 --> 00:27:04,600
They're not seeing the team.

477
00:27:04,600 --> 00:27:06,920
They're not seeing the team actually producing anything.

478
00:27:06,920 --> 00:27:11,280
And so when you don't have visibility into what's being produced, you find something

479
00:27:11,280 --> 00:27:16,100
else that you can manage, which is numbers and estimates and things like that.

480
00:27:16,100 --> 00:27:21,260
I'm not saying those things aren't important in a lot of contexts, but rapid and frequent

481
00:27:21,260 --> 00:27:23,020
delivery trumps all of that.

482
00:27:23,020 --> 00:27:29,500
If you are consistently having conversations with people in the business, or if you're

483
00:27:29,500 --> 00:27:33,820
a product owner and you're working with a team and you're consistently seeing work coming

484
00:27:33,820 --> 00:27:34,360
out of that team.

485
00:27:34,360 --> 00:27:37,360
It's a lot easier to make the case for we're moving forward.

486
00:27:37,360 --> 00:27:40,740
Yes, we're tracking towards that.

487
00:27:40,740 --> 00:27:46,240
As with all language learning, we made a lot of mistakes in this.

