00:42:49,540 --> 00:42:55,920
harder. That work would be so much harder to do. It was a lost opportunity to refine

717
00:42:55,920 --> 00:43:00,940
the model and improve it. Why does this matter?

718
00:43:00,940 --> 00:43:03,860
Why does this matter?

719
00:43:04,820 --> 00:43:19,820
There's a lot of teams that I see that go very fast, and they make a lot of progress

720
00:43:19,820 --> 00:43:22,220
very quickly on something new.

721
00:43:22,220 --> 00:43:26,980
But then over time, it starts to get harder and harder, and they slow down.

722
00:43:26,980 --> 00:43:32,780
And the speed that they were able to crank out functionality at the beginning is not

723
00:43:32,780 --> 00:43:35,080
there anymore.

724
00:43:35,080 --> 00:43:39,760
They become more and more mired in what they were creating, and it becomes more and more

725
00:43:39,760 --> 00:43:50,080
of an effort of duct taping what they've done and writing workarounds for what they've done.

726
00:43:50,080 --> 00:43:56,280
And they don't get the benefit of investing in design as they go along.

727
00:43:56,280 --> 00:44:02,320
The idea is to make design part of your development culture.

728
00:44:02,320 --> 00:44:02,760
A lot of...

729
00:44:02,760 --> 00:44:07,560
A lot of organizations are very good at UX and product management in terms of building

730
00:44:07,560 --> 00:44:11,900
the right thing and building it in a way that is a really great experience for the user.

731
00:44:11,900 --> 00:44:14,980
What DDD brings is more that...

732
00:44:14,980 --> 00:44:19,140
It's a little bit more hidden, is that design that is happening that informs everything

733
00:44:19,140 --> 00:44:21,160
else that the team is doing.

734
00:44:21,160 --> 00:44:25,600
It's having a consistent set of concepts that represent that.

735
00:44:25,600 --> 00:44:30,660
And the goal, as I mentioned in the abstract, the goal is not to say, well, we're doing

736
00:44:30,660 --> 00:44:31,660
DDD.

737
00:44:31,660 --> 00:44:32,660
You know?

738
00:44:32,660 --> 00:44:33,660
We're doing design.

739
00:44:33,660 --> 00:44:34,660
That's not the goal.

740
00:44:34,660 --> 00:44:36,000
That's the means to the end.

741
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:43,620
The end is to deliver the functionality and to meet the needs of the users.

742
00:44:43,620 --> 00:44:50,220
So what's your journey going to look like?

743
00:44:50,220 --> 00:44:56,160
I mentioned some things that work for us.

744
00:44:56,160 --> 00:45:02,460
One of the things that I think, as I look back, was incredibly valuable for us was the

745
00:45:02,460 --> 00:45:03,460
idea of prioritizing making over analysis.

746
00:45:03,460 --> 00:45:04,460
You know what I mean by that?

747
00:45:04,460 --> 00:45:15,940
There's a tendency, and I feel this sometimes, is to want to make things perfect and to not

748
00:45:15,940 --> 00:45:20,340
move forward until I have a really good understanding of something.

749
00:45:20,340 --> 00:45:26,400
And with domain-driven design, the idea is what you should try and do is to take what

750
00:45:26,400 --> 00:45:30,460
you understand and make something, and then get some feedback on that, learn from that,

751
00:45:30,460 --> 00:45:31,460
and expand on it as you go.

752
00:45:31,460 --> 00:45:32,460
That's the goal.

753
00:45:32,460 --> 00:45:38,040
That's that iterative, collaborative aspect of design.

754
00:45:38,040 --> 00:45:43,060
Rather than getting paralyzed with trying to analyze it and get it perfect, is learn

755
00:45:43,060 --> 00:45:47,560
enough of it to be able to make something, get some feedback on it, and move forward,

756
00:45:47,560 --> 00:45:48,760
because you're learning as you're going.

757
00:45:48,760 --> 00:45:50,160
It's the same way with learning a language.

758
00:45:50,160 --> 00:45:55,540
You have to practice speaking it to be able to make progress.

